{"kind":"request","text":"Design page: the kernel boundary of dap. What is kernel, what is a platform service, what is an application, and the rule for adding to the kernel.\n\nWhy now (Hugh, 22 October 2026). Hugh asked to look at dap as a new operating system, a place where custom-built, long-lived applications run and interact with services, while the system is in a research phase, and to apply the operating-system lessons that matter now. The first lesson: the systems that lasted kept the kernel to isolation, ordering and message passing, ran every other service as an ordinary program, and separated mechanism from policy. The research systems that put policy in the kernel, or let the kernel grow with each feature, were rebuilt instead of extended. dap has no written boundary today. This page writes it down so that each later design can be checked against it.\n\nWhat docs/architecture.md says today. Every part of a dap application is a scope; a scope pins one definition; the judges and the fold live in dap-derive and are pure; applyEntry is the only code that changes state; the runtime and the verifier call the same functions; scopes compose by delivery only. That is a kernel description, and it is a good one. The same page also says that the platform's own definitions (membership, register, directory, rules, destination, inbox) are data with marks whose rules are code in packages/platform, and that a verifier needs that code and lists it under what it trusts. That is policy inside the kernel. The guard grammar request e949b0df found a second copy of the kernel's grammar (derive validate mirrors the runtime judge). The consolidation survey and the history note (notes/2026-10-20-history-out-of-the-readable-tree.md) found the mass of the tree in shipped platform versions, v1 forms and lane generations, each of which is kernel surface until it is retired on purpose.\n\nCandidate classes, for the page to confirm or correct. Kernel: packages/contract (entry and act forms, ScopeRef, incarnation, fact references), packages/derive (judges, fold, rule expressions), packages/scope (the turn, delivery, dispatcher), packages/replay (the verifier), and the identity of a scope and a key. Platform services: packages/platform (register, directory, membership, rules, destination, inbox, archive binding, floor, extents, versions), packages/lanes, packages/checkers, packages/package-graph, packages/git, packages/bytes, the hosted MCP endpoint in packages/mcp, and the room host. Applications: apps/counter, apps/jam, apps/room's declared parts, examples/grow, examples/lending, examples/demo-repo, and the packages the sales, market and matter notes design. Tools: packages/cli, packages/client. The page should state for each platform service whether it is already an application in the sense of docs/write-an-application.md (a definition pinned in a scope) or still a special case the kernel knows about, and for each special case what retiring it would take.\n\nWhat the page must carry. (1) The three classes with every part placed, and the test applied. (2) The kernel's promises in one list: the record is the interface; every entry form ever written stays replayable by the same derivation code; the definition schema is versioned; a judgment is about one head; delivery is the only channel between scopes; a refusal carries its reason; the sequence is the clock; a scope URI resolves. The definition-succession note (request 95d01d30) adds its principle to this list when it lands, and the one-channel audit (filed today) adds its principle. (3) The rule: nothing new enters the kernel without a review that cites this page and says why the part cannot be a platform service or an application. (4) The parts that fail the test today, each with what passing would take, as candidates for later requests, not filed by this page. (5) A note that the second host (notes/2026-10-19-self-hosting-on-celld.md) is the forcing function that keeps host details out of the kernel, and which host details are in it now.\n\nScope. Document only. No code moves. Implementation requests, if any, follow the page's landing and are filed one at a time.","body":{"conditions":"Document only: one page under docs/ (or one section of docs/architecture.md if the planner prefers) with a status line, linked from docs/README.md and the roadmap. Plain English per ISO 24495-1 for a technical audience; no product names other than GitHub. The page lists every package under packages/, every app under apps/ and every example under examples/ in exactly one of three classes: kernel, platform service, application. It states the test it applied to each part (can this part be retired or replaced with no change to packages/contract and packages/derive, and no change to the meaning of any recorded entry) and names each part that fails the test today with what it would take to pass. It states the kernel's promises in one list, including that every entry form ever written stays replayable and that the definition schema is versioned, and the rule that nothing enters the kernel without a review that cites this page. Checker review before landing; land after approval.","priority":"after the definition-succession note 95d01d30; cheap, mostly writing","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}